  "summary": "Imaging-derived, subject-specific geometry is now driving organ-level physiological simulations across multiple organ systems.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is subject-specific geometry derived from medical imaging as the substrate for organ-level computational models. A 1D flow framework applied to CT-derived pulmonary arteries simulates lung resection, and its wave intensity changes agree with a clinical study. A porcine ventricular digital twin integrates multi-modal data to simulate healthy and location-dependent infarcted electrophysiology and ECGs. A semi-automatic MRI workflow (3D Slicer, Python, thin-plate spline landmark mapping) builds personalized musculoskeletal models and reports that personalized geometry matters in healthy adults. The novelty lies in pairing imaging-derived anatomy with physiological simulation across pulmonary, cardiac, and musculoskeletal systems.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-03-18"
